  <h1>What to Do After Defamation</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>1. Preserve the evidence immediately</h2><p>Screenshot and archive the defamatory statement with timestamps before it is deleted. Use archive.org or a screen recording tool. Defamatory posts are frequently taken down &mdash; once gone, proving the statement becomes much harder.</p>
  <h2>2. Document harm</h2><p>Keep records of lost contracts, job offers withdrawn, reduced business income, and medical treatment for emotional distress. These form the basis of actual damages. If colleagues, employers, or clients reacted negatively to the statement, document their communications.</p>
  <h2>3. Consider a retraction demand</h2><p>Many states require (or incentivize) a pre-suit retraction demand. In some states, failure to demand a retraction before filing limits recovery to actual damages only &mdash; no presumed or punitive damages. A retraction demand also establishes the defendant&rsquo;s knowledge of the falsity, supporting an actual malice finding later.</p>
  <h2>4. Watch the statute of limitations</h2><p>Typically 1&ndash;3 years from publication. Some states use the discovery rule; most use the single-publication rule (clock starts at first publication). Do not wait &mdash; early action preserves evidence and forces the defendant to address the false statement promptly.</p>
  <h2>5. Consult a defamation attorney</h2><p>Defamation law is highly state-specific and heavily influenced by First Amendment doctrine. An attorney can assess the actual malice requirement, evaluate defenses, and advise whether a demand letter or lawsuit is the right strategy.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-defamation-damages-work">how damages work</a> and <a href="/guides/defamation-misconceptions">common misconceptions</a>.</p>
